1. What is APT?

APT stands for Advanced Package Tool.

It is the package manager used in Ubuntu and Debian to:

Install software
Update software
Remove software
Manage software packages

2. Real-Life Example

Imagine your mobile phone.

Step 1

You open the Google Play Store.

The Play Store checks:

"Are there any new versions available?"

This is like:

sudo apt update

Nothing is downloaded or installed.

Step 2

You click Update All.

The apps are downloaded and updated.

This is like:

sudo apt upgrade

3. Definition 
a) apt update
What does it do?

apt update downloads the latest package information from the configured software repositories.

It does NOT update any installed software.

It only updates the package index (database).

WORKING 
When you run this command, APT contacts the repositories listed in:

/etc/apt/sources.list

and

/etc/apt/sources.list.d/

It downloads the latest package metadata, including:

Package names
Available versions
Dependencies
Security updates

b) apt upgrade installs the latest versions of already installed packages using the information downloaded by apt update.

4. Related Commands
Install Software
sudo apt install nginx

Remove Software
sudo apt remove nginx

Remove Package with Configuration Files
sudo apt purge nginx

Remove Unused Dependencies
sudo apt autoremove

Clean Downloaded Package Cache
sudo apt clean

Note:-

1. What is DNF?

DNF stands for Dandified YUM.

It is the default package manager used in:

RHEL 8 & RHEL 9
Rocky Linux
AlmaLinux
Fedora
Oracle Linux (newer versions)

2. Why Do We Need DNF?

Suppose you want to install Nginx.

Without DNF,

You would have to:

Download RPM manually.
Download all required dependencies.
Install them one by one.

This is difficult.

DNF automatically:

Downloads the package.
Downloads required dependencies.
Installs everything.

DNF is used to:

Install software
Update software
Remove software
Search software packages
Manage package dependencies

When you install software,

DNF downloads it from a repository.

3. What is a Package?

A package is a software application.

| Package | Purpose            |
| ------- | ------------------ |
| nginx   | Web Server         |
| httpd   | Apache Web Server  |
| vim     | Text Editor        |
| git     | Version Control    |
| docker  | Container Platform |

4. What is a Repository?

A repository (repo) is a storage location where Linux packages are kept.

Think of it as an online software store.

5. Related commands

a) Check Configured Repositories
dnf repolist

b) Install a Package
Syntax
sudo dnf install package_name

Example
sudo dnf install git

c) Search for a Package
dnf search nginx

d) View Package Information
dnf info nginx

e) Remove a Package
sudo dnf remove nginx

DNF removes:

nginx
Unneeded dependencies (if applicable)

f) List Installed Packages
dnf list installed

List Available Packages
dnf list available

Check for Available Updates
dnf check-update

g) Install Multiple Packages
sudo dnf install git vim wget

DNF installs all three packages.
